    In words: the abstract set WireGuiGame must fill shrank from 37 to 28 (27
    from IGuiGame plus AbstractGuiGame's own updateCurrentPlayer); NO new
    abstract member appeared. tempShowZones, hideZones and restoreOldZones are
    deleted; openZones is a default void with no backupLastZones flag;
    showManaPool, hideManaPool, updateShards and the overlay pair became
    defaults (their overrides still compile and are kept);
    IGameController.passPriority() is gone and InputPassPriority gained a
    public passPriority(). Snap.java reads none of the CardView getters that
    went away, and all three hard-rule-8 gates (canBeShownTo,
    canFaceDownBeShownTo, getAlternateState inside the one alt block) are
    present on the snapshot.

WHAT IT CHANGES: 2 files. +64/-84 lines with comments; +18/-59 lines of code.
    WireGuiGame.java  deletes tempShowZones, hideZones, restoreOldZones and the
                      now-uncalled removeZoneHints; openZones becomes the
                      3-argument default-void override and still pushes the
                      input.openZones hint (the exile path that survives);
                      act passPriority goes through InputPassPriority.
                      passPriority(), because IGameController.passPriority()
                      is gone (and InputPassPriority.onOk() now first accepts
                      a pending yield suggestion, so OK is not a substitute).
    RecordMatch.java  the same three deletions and the openZones signature,
                      in SpectatorGui.
    Untouched: Snap.java, Bootstrap, HeadlessGuiBase's thread names, MtgTable's
    CLI, tools/lint-bridge.sh, tools/check-forge.sh, config.json and
    docs/protocol.md. No wire shape changes. MtgTable's run summary still says
    "members fired = N of 37" -- cosmetic; fix to 28 on the day, with the
    match-setup worktree that owns that file.
    Known loss: the {hand} input.openZones hint from InputSelectEntitiesFromList
    (23 frames of 4 committed fixtures) no longer arrives -- the seam path that
    produced it is gone. Restoring it from Snap's per-id zones is a new decision.

